, and a Democratic-tilting national environment plus Johnson's primary momentum leave some room for a closer race"], "rationale": "Every available signal points the same direction: the state's Republican lean, two Democratic-sponsored polls showing Wilson up eight, unanimous Solid R ratings, and prediction markets pricing Johnson at five to six percent. Johnson's primary upset and active fall campaign give Democrats a path to a narrower result, but not yet to a genuinely competitive one.", "watch": "The scheduled October debates and any fresh post-Labor Day polling will show whether Johnson is closing the gap or the race is settling at the state's usual Republican margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 46 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Open seat: Henry McMaster (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's governorship is open this year as term-limited Henry McMaster steps aside, and Republican Alan Wilson faces Democratic state representative Jermaine Johnson. The state's eight-point Republican lean anchors the race, and the early evidence points the same way: the only two public surveys, both conducted by Democratic-leaning pollsters over the summer, showed Wilson ahead by eight points, and prediction markets price Johnson's chances at just five to six percent.\n\nNational race raters are unanimous in calling the contest solidly Republican, and Wilson enters the fall with the structural advantages that come with his party's standing in South Carolina. Johnson has drawn attention since his primary upset, campaigning on a 'New SC' agenda with a faith tour and clergy support, and the candidates meet in debates through October. The race rates as Likely Republican, with movement possible if the debates or newer polling show Johnson gaining ground.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0588, "margin": -9.1}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0546, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0722, "m